<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rabban Sauma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1220–<a href="/years/1294">1294</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Rabban Bar Sauma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rabban Sauma was a Turkic monk of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>), who travelled west on pilgrimage and became an envoy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a>. In 1287–1288 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> and Pope Nicholas IV, to seek an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. His account of the journey survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabban-sauma.jpg" alt="Rabban Sauma" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VoyagesOfRabbanBarSauma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rabban Bar Sauma was born around 1220 in or near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, then called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a>. Bar Hebraeus gives his origin as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a>, while Chinese accounts give his people as the Öngüd, a Turkic people counted in the Mongol caste under Yuan law. His name is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> for Son of Fasting, although his family was wealthy.</p><p>A Christian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>, he became an ascetic monk at about the age of 20 and taught for decades. In middle age he set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> with a younger student, Rabban Markos, travelling by way of Khotan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maragha">Maragha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. Warnings of danger on the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> turned them back to the Mongol-controlled Persia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, where they remained.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Markos was elected patriarch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> as Yahballaha III in 1281, and some years later proposed his former teacher as an envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Ilkhan Arghun wanted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-mongol-alliance">Franco-Mongol alliance</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Bar Sauma left in 1287 with gifts, letters, a large retinue and 30 riding animals, and Europeans spoke to him in Persian. He was received by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronicus II Palaeologus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, praised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, and from a ship saw the eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Mount Etna</a> on 18 June 1287.</p><p>He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> too late to meet Pope Honorius IV, spent the winter of 1287–1288 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, and then spent a month with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>, who was friendly but could not join a military alliance because of conflicts with the Welsh and the Scots. On his return to Rome the newly elected Pope Nicholas IV gave him communion on Palm Sunday 1288.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bar Sauma returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1288 with letters and gifts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a>'s answers followed in 1289, but the alliance came to nothing and was abandoned, though the contacts encouraged communication and trade between East and West, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> later sent missionaries such as Giovanni da Montecorvino to the Mongol court. He spent his last years in Baghdad and died there in 1294.</p><p>His account of his travels, probably written there, survives in the History of Mar Yahballaha and Rabban Sauma, and is valued as a view of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> by an observer from the East. It has been translated into English by James A. Montgomery in 1927 and E. A. Wallis Budge in 1928.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rabban Bar Sauma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Embassies to European monarchs and the papacy, including the mission of the monk <b>Rabban Sauma</b> in 1287–1288, sought a Franco-Mongol alliance against the Mamluks, though these efforts never produced effective military co-operation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pax Mongolica allowed travellers such as Marco Polo, the Moroccan Ibn Battuta and the Nestorian monk <b>Rabban Sauma</b> to cross Eurasia in relative safety, and made possible a commercial and cultural exchange that would not be matched until the age of European maritime expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Daoists lost ground to the Buddhists, while Christians of the Church of the East, such as the monk <b>Rabban Sauma</b>, had an office of their own.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.87,116.33&amp;year=1268" aria-label="Zhongdu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Rabban Sauma"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4759 4156h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4759 4156h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.87,116.33&amp;year=1268">What was here in 1268? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
